#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193606} :activity/id #uuid "263de223-cabb-58ff-aad3-1a84df02f52a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050854 :topic/id #uuid "6c0cdb45-4687-5b06-beee-a13677429ccb" :topic/math-academy-id 4037} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193861 :tutorial/content "When drawing angles expressed in radians, it can be helpful to convert the angle to degrees first. This is especially helpful when writing trigonometric ratios in terms of reference angles, as we usually need to determine which quadrant the angle lies in.\n\nLet's express the following trigonometric ratio in terms of a reference angle:\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nYou might find it tricky to visualize this angle straight off the bat. So, let's start by converting the angle measure into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = \n\t120^\\circ.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ is equivalent to $120^\\circ,$ which lies in the $2$nd quadrant.\n\n![](images/34/342602449228f93042121b22f39d6cc203a6d945934a9ddb726e862838832815.png)\n\nNow, to express $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)$ in terms of $\\sin\\theta_R,$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle $\\theta_R.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for $\\theta_R.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle $\\theta_R$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{2\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{3}-\\frac{2\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\sin\\theta_R = \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)$ must be *positive* because the sine ratio is *always* positive in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "4923e69b-5a86-59ae-ab93-5cd5e68e7ef0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6765 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f104bf84-a7fb-5c40-a540-f70c2282aa70" :step/math-academy-id 41036 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193607}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193607 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192749 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192468 :question/id #uuid "2f02b5d4-640f-5212-af28-66c572d47b37" :question/math-academy-id "e-10828"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "961a5c28-9e1e-5b0c-bd04-63068bebd8b4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051043}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567112}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Sine of an Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle in Radians"} :step/id #uuid "eb6eb779-446c-51b6-aa71-efb894db7bee" :step/math-academy-id 41037 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193608}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193608 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192750 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192469 :question/id #uuid "0a1428c6-96e8-5c82-82b2-ed51375454ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-10829"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a7d5897-3360-59fb-9f64-2f9f641290be" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567089